.page head 1 write the first part of files

# SYNOPSIS
*head* [*-qv*] [*-n* _N_ | *-c* _N_ | *-*_N_] [_FILE_...]

# DESCRIPTION
`head` writes the first ten lines of each _FILE_ to standard output. Without a file, or for the file name `-`, it reads standard input. When several files are given each one is introduced by a line `==> NAME <==`.

# OPTIONS
@ *-n* _N_
    Write the first _N_ lines. If _N_ is negative, write everything except the last _N_ lines.
@ *-c* _N_
    Write the first _N_ bytes instead of lines.
@ *-*_N_
    A shorthand for `-n` _N_ (`head -5`).
@ *-q*
    Never write the `==> NAME <==` headers.
@ *-v*
    Always write them, even for a single file.

A count may end in `k` or `K` (times 1024), `m` or `M` (times 1048576) or `b` (times 512).

# EXAMPLES
    head -3 /Hakurei/etc/passwd
    ls -l /bin | head -n 20
    head -c 16 /dev/random | xxd
    head -n -1 notes.txt

# EXIT STATUS
0, or 1 for a bad option or count. A file that cannot be opened is reported and the exit status is 1.

# SEE ALSO
tail(1), cat(1), sed(1)
